{"name":"Polar Wind","hex":"#b4dfed","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"polar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or having a pole or polarity, as:\n Of, relating to, measured from, or referred to a geographic pole (the North Pole or South Pole); within the Arctic or Antarctic circles.\n\n Of an orbit that passes over, or near, one of these poles.\n Having a dipole; ionic.\n Of a coordinate system, specifying the location of a point in a plane by using a radius and an angle.\n Having but two possible answers, yes and no.\n Of or relating to a pole (extreme) on any spectrum or field, such as an ideologically pure dogmatic position as opposed to any syncretic integration or balancing of competing principles.","Of, relating to, measured from, or referred to a geographic pole (the North Pole or South Pole); within the Arctic or Antarctic circles."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The line joining the points of contact of tangents drawn to meet a curve from a point called the pole of the line."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/polar"},{"term":"wind","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Real or perceived movement of atmospheric air usually caused by convection or differences in air pressure.","Air artificially put in motion by any force or action."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To blow air through a wind instrument or horn to make a sound.","To cause (someone) to become breathless, as by a blow to the abdomen, or by physical exertion, running, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wind"}],"entries":[{"title":"Polar wind","blocks":[{"text":"The polar wind or plasma fountain is a permanent outflow of plasma from the polar regions of Earth's magnetosphere. Conceptually similar to the solar wind, it is one of several mechanisms for the outflow of ionized particles. Ions accelerated by a polarization electric field known as an ambipolar electric field is believed to be the primary cause of polar wind. Similar processes operate on other planets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Earth_cutaway.png/330px-Earth_cutaway.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polar_wind","wikidata":"Q7209106"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind","wikidata":"Q8094"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Plasma_fountain.gif/330px-Plasma_fountain.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Plasma_fountain.gif/330px-Plasma_fountain.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Earth's plasma fountain, showing oxygen, helium, and hydrogen ions which gush into space from regions near the Earth's poles. The faint yellow area shown above the north pole represents gas lost from Earth into space; the green area is the aurora borealis—or plasma energy pouring back into the atmosphere.[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlasma_fountain.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Discovering_Earth%E2%80%99s_Third_Global_Energy_Field_%28SVS14628_-_2B%29.jpg/500px-Discovering_Earth%E2%80%99s_Third_Global_Energy_Field_%28SVS14628_-_2B%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Discovering_Earth%E2%80%99s_Third_Global_Energy_Field_%28SVS14628_-_2B%29.jpg/1280px-Discovering_Earth%E2%80%99s_Third_Global_Energy_Field_%28SVS14628_-_2B%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Discovering_Earth%E2%80%99s_Third_Global_Energy_Field_%28SVS14628_-_2B%29.jpg/500px-Discovering_Earth%E2%80%99s_Third_Global_Energy_Field_%28SVS14628_-_2B%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Conceptual diagram of the two main effects of the ambipolar electric field: inflating the ionosphere and generating the polar wind.The sparkling blue haze surrounding Earth represents the plasma in the ionosphere. The sparkling lines represent polar wind flowing up and out.[12]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiscovering_Earth%E2%80%99s_Third_Global_Energy_Field_(SVS14628_-_2B).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png/500px-10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png/500px-10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surface analysis of the Great Blizzard of 1888. Areas with greater isobaric packing indicate higher winds.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Anemometer_2745.JPG/500px-Anemometer_2745.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut